{"name":"Ice Cream Cone","hex":"#e3d0bf","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ice cream cone","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An edible holder, usually sweet and usually a baked good, for ice cream.","A thin, conical confection baked from crepe batter, sealed at its apex, used to hold ice cream while it is being eaten."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ice_cream_cone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ice cream cone","blocks":[{"text":"An ice cream cone or poke is a brittle, cone-shaped pastry, usually made of a wafer similar in texture to a waffle, made so ice cream can be carried and eaten without a bowl or spoon. Many styles of cones are made, including Cornetto cones, sugar-coated and chocolate-coated cones. The term ice cream cone can also refer, informally, to the cone with one or more scoops of ice cream on top."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Strawberry_ice_cream_cone_%285076899310%29.jpg/330px-Strawberry_ice_cream_cone_%285076899310%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice_cream_cone","wikidata":"Q1156634"},{"title":"Ice cream","blocks":[{"text":"Ice cream is a frozen dessert typically made from milk or cream that has been flavoured with a sweetener, either sugar or an alternative, and a spice, such as cocoa or vanilla, or with fruit, such as strawberries or peaches. Food colouring is sometimes added in addition to stabilizers. The mixture is cooled below the freezing point of water and stirred to incorporate air spaces and prevent detectable ice crystals from forming. It can also be made by whisking a flavoured cream base and liquid nitrogen together. The result is a smooth, semi-solid foam that is solid at very low temperatures. It becomes more malleable as its temperature increases."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Vanilla_Ice_Cream_Cone_at_Camp_Manitoulin.jpg/330px-Vanilla_Ice_Cream_Cone_at_Camp_Manitoulin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice_cream","wikidata":"Q13233"},{"title":"Ice","blocks":[{"text":"Ice is water that is frozen into a solid state, typically forming at or below temperatures of 0 °C, 32 °F, or 273.15 K. It occurs naturally on Earth, on other planets, in Oort cloud objects, and as interstellar ice. As a naturally occurring crystalline inorganic solid with an ordered structure, ice is considered to be a mineral. Depending on the presence of impurities such as particles of soil or bubbles of air, it can appear transparent or a more or less opaque bluish-white color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg/330px-Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice","wikidata":"Q23392"},{"title":"Cream","blocks":[{"text":"Cream is a dairy product composed of the higher-fat layer skimmed from the top of milk before homogenization. The butterfat, which is less dense, rises to the top and is skimmed off, resulting in gravity cream. The industrial production of cream instead uses centrifugal separators to make separator cream. Cream is often sold in grades defined by their butterfat content. It contains high levels of saturated fat, in ascending proportion from light cream to heavy cream."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/01_Mmm..._Apple_Crisp_with_Whipped_Cream.jpg/330px-01_Mmm..._Apple_Crisp_with_Whipped_Cream.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cream","wikidata":"Q13228"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Francatelli%27s_Modern_Cook_-_Iced_Pudding%2C_a_la_Chesterfield_%28detail%29.jpg/500px-Francatelli%27s_Modern_Cook_-_Iced_Pudding%2C_a_la_Chesterfield_%28detail%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Francatelli%27s_Modern_Cook_-_Iced_Pudding%2C_a_la_Chesterfield_%28detail%29.jpg/500px-Francatelli%27s_Modern_Cook_-_Iced_Pudding%2C_a_la_Chesterfield_%28detail%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Iced Pudding, a la Chesterfield, in  Charles Elmé Francatelli's The Modern Cook, first published in 1846. The illustration is one of the earliest to show something akin to ice cream cones, arranged around the base of the iced dessert. Francatelli described the cones as \"gauffres, filled with some of the ice cream\".","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFrancatelli's_Modern_Cook_-_Iced_Pudding%2C_a_la_Chesterfield_(detail).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Historic_Trademarks_-_Ice_Cream_Sandwich.jpg/500px-Historic_Trademarks_-_Ice_Cream_Sandwich.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Historic_Trademarks_-_Ice_Cream_Sandwich.jpg/1280px-Historic_Trademarks_-_Ice_Cream_Sandwich.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Historic_Trademarks_-_Ice_Cream_Sandwich.jpg/500px-Historic_Trademarks_-_Ice_Cream_Sandwich.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Ice Cream Sandwich or Ice Cream Cornucopia trademark was registered with the state of Missouri and introduced at the St. Louis World's Fair in 1904.[10]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHistoric_Trademarks_-_Ice_Cream_Sandwich.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Scan_of_%22Romance_of_the_Ice_Cream_Cone%22_article%2C_Page_1%2C_Western_Conf